1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:05,669
Have you ever wondered how life has changed since the very beginning?

2
00:00:06,419 --> 00:00:10,707
How a planet of rock and water became home to cells, animals and minds?

3
00:00:11,457 --> 00:00:17,559
Nobody was there to watch. But we can build a small world, give it simple rules, and see what happens.

4
00:00:18,309 --> 00:00:23,109
This portal lets you explore every step of that journey, and shows what we know so far.

5
00:00:23,859 --> 00:00:28,595
To keep the scale in mind, let’s squeeze the whole history of Earth into a single day.

6
00:00:29,345 --> 00:00:34,891
At midnight, the planet forms from dust and fire. We humans arrive in the last six seconds.

7
00:00:37,941 --> 00:00:41,184
Within the first hours, the planet cools and oceans fill.

8
00:00:41,934 --> 00:00:48,974
Tides wash over the rocks and leave pools that dry in the sun. On the deep floor, hot vents leak heat and minerals.

9
00:00:49,724 --> 00:00:54,311
In a drying pool, molecules crowd together, and some join into chains.

10
00:00:55,061 --> 00:01:00,607
This is our simulation of that young coast. Every patch of water is calculated as it runs.

11
00:01:01,357 --> 00:01:03,533
Here is the heat, glowing at the vents.

12
00:01:04,283 --> 00:01:09,297
And here are the chains. They appear in drying pools, and fade when the tide comes back.

13
00:01:10,047 --> 00:01:16,553
A few chains help copy themselves. Nothing is alive yet, but something has started to pass itself on.

14
00:01:20,603 --> 00:01:25,745
By four or five in the morning, there are cells: a thin skin, with instructions inside.

15
00:01:26,495 --> 00:01:32,639
A cell eats, grows and splits in two. Each daughter copies the instructions, with small mistakes.

16
00:01:33,389 --> 00:01:37,719
In our world, the first cells have no working motor. They drift with the currents.

17
00:01:38,469 --> 00:01:43,184
But a cell with a slightly better motor reaches more food, and leaves more daughters.

18
00:01:43,934 --> 00:01:51,123
In a few generations, the motor gene rises about eight times, then holds at a best strength. Nobody designed it.

19
00:01:55,173 --> 00:02:00,912
For hundreds of millions of years, microbes build mats and domes in the shallows, layer by layer.

20
00:02:01,662 --> 00:02:03,625
Then some cells start eating others.

21
00:02:04,375 --> 00:02:07,959
In our simulation, big red predators swallow anything small.

22
00:02:08,709 --> 00:02:12,570
A daughter that stays stuck to its mother makes a clump too big to swallow.

23
00:02:13,320 --> 00:02:20,872
So the gene for sticking spreads. It rises up to about five times in our run, even though clumped cells must share their food.

24
00:02:24,922 --> 00:02:28,506
Then, for most of the day, microbes rule the planet alone.

25
00:02:29,256 --> 00:02:36,509
Before noon, some of them fill the air with oxygen. It poisons many others, and changes the sky and seas for ever.

26
00:02:37,259 --> 00:02:41,057
In the evening, ice spreads almost to the equator, then melts again.

27
00:02:41,807 --> 00:02:44,303
Only after that do large bodies appear.

28
00:02:47,353 --> 00:02:49,806
Cells that stay together start to share the work.

29
00:02:50,556 --> 00:02:58,385
Some squeeze, like the first muscle cells. Some only make new cells. And some pass signals: a net of nerves, with no centre.

30
00:02:59,135 --> 00:03:05,941
In our world, a body moves only when its squeezing cells work in step. Out of step, it just sloshes in place.

31
00:03:06,691 --> 00:03:11,384
Nerve cells carry one cell’s taste of food to the whole body, so it turns as one.

32
00:03:15,434 --> 00:03:21,023
Around nine in the evening, the sea fills with animals: eyes, shells, legs and jaws.

33
00:03:21,773 --> 00:03:26,104
Hunters and hunted push each other to change, faster than ever before.

34
00:03:26,854 --> 00:03:33,190
Our creatures descend from those bodies. Each has a small brain of neurons that learns what is good to eat.

35
00:03:33,940 --> 00:03:38,313
Prey that notice hunters in time survive. Hunters that chase well eat.

36
00:03:39,063 --> 00:03:45,591
Too many hunters, and they empty the sea and starve. Balance is not given. It emerges, or it fails.

37
00:03:49,641 --> 00:03:52,671
Here is the big idea behind everything you just saw.

38
00:03:53,421 --> 00:03:59,159
Nothing in this world is told what to do. There is no plan, no designer, no one in control.

39
00:03:59,909 --> 00:04:09,637
Each molecule, cell and creature follows a few local rules about what is right next to it: react, eat, copy with small mistakes, stick or let go.

40
00:04:10,387 --> 00:04:15,379
Swimming, colonies, bodies, brains and balance are not written anywhere. They emerge.

41
00:04:16,129 --> 00:04:19,351
And it is all one world, Gaia, seen at different levels.

42
00:04:20,101 --> 00:04:25,498
Now it’s your turn. Dive into the planet, change the rules in a lab, and see what emerges.

